    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d789">

                <pb facs="0799" n="795" sameAs="frus:frus1939v03/pg795"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94112/287: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-12-21T19:00:00+09:00">December 21,
                            1939—7 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received December 21—1:30 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>697. Our 694, December 20, 2 p.m.<anchor n="22" xml:id="d789-f1"/> I refer to the
                    third of the five points enumerated to my British colleague<anchor n="23"
                        xml:id="d789-f2"/> by the Vice Minister<anchor n="24" xml:id="d789-f3"/>
                    which are to be discussed in connection with the opening of the Yangtze.
                    Information was brought to me recently that Jardine, Matheson<anchor n="25"
                        xml:id="d789-f4"/> have been in touch with a number of Japanese interests
                    concerning a Japanese proposal for pooling foreign shipping on the Yangtze, and
                    that the former were interested in the proposals, especially if American
                    shipping is brought into the pool. I reported the matter by despatch in view of
                    assurances by Craigie that he had advised <pb facs="0800" n="796" xml:id="pg796"
                    /> Jardine against present participation, but reference by Tani to the project
                    indicates that it has assumed important proportions.</p>
                <p>My despatch will go forward in the pouch leaving December 23.<anchor n="26"
                        xml:id="d789-f5"/>
                </p>
                <p>Sent to the Department, repeated to Shanghai.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d789-f1" n="22" xml:id="d789-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it gave a paraphrase of a telegram from the British Ambassador in
                    Japan to his Government. The substance of this telegram is given in the British
                    Embassy’s <hi rendition="#italic">aide-mémoire</hi> of December 26, <ref
                        target="#pg797">p. 797</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d789-f2" n="23" xml:id="d789-f2-content">
                <p>Sir Robert L. Craigie, British Ambassador in Japan.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d789-f3" n="24" xml:id="d789-f3-content">
                <p>Masaynki Tani.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d789-f4" n="25" xml:id="d789-f4-content">
                <p>British shipping firm in China.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d789-f5" n="26" xml:id="d789-f5-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
